<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sepúlveda</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sepúlveda is a small historic town in the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/segovia">Segovia</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, set on a rock above the gorges of the Duratón. Fortified by the counts of Castile in the tenth century, it received in 1076 from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-vi-of-castile">Alfonso VI</a> a fuero, or local charter, granting settlers freedom, land and self-government. The fuero of Sepúlveda became a model for the settlement of the lands between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/douro">Duero</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tagus">Tagus</a>.</p></div>
